introduction:
Robots plan and simulate on convex collision geometry. Sampling-based motion planners [1, 2], rigid-body simulators [3, 4, 5, 6], and contact-rich manipulation pipelines all rely on fast, robust proximity and penetration queries between convex bodies, computed with algorithms such as GJK and EPA [7, 8]. Objects are therefore represented as convex decompositions, i.e., unions of convex parts that approximate a triangle mesh, and production simulators routinely build them with tools such as V-HACD [9] and CoACD [10].

Image-to-3D generative models now offer a direct route from a camera image to 3D geometry. The strongest methods sample a high-quality shape latent with a large image-conditioned diffusion or flow-matching model in a single feed-forward pass [11, 12, 13], and robotics increasingly uses them to build scene geometry for manipulation and planning [14, 15, 16]. However, these models are optimized for visual fidelity, not physical simulation. They output dense triangle meshes (∼106{\sim}10^{6} faces) with small but near-ubiquitous topological defects such as non-manifold edges and stray fragments (quantified in §IV-A). Turning such a mesh into collision geometry takes a fragile chain of decimation [17], repair [18], watertightening, and approximate convex decomposition. This reconstruct-then-decompose workflow [15] is slow and sensitive to errors at every stage. Skipping it is worse, because it does not fail loudly: as we show in §IV-B, physics engines accept the raw mesh and silently substitute a single convex hull, producing a plausible-looking scene with the wrong collision shape.

We propose I2CD (Image-to-Convex-Decomposition), which bypasses this workflow by predicting the convex decomposition directly from a single RGB image (Fig. 1). Our key insight is that the generator’s mesh is the wrong output but its features are the right input: a pretrained image-to-3D model already encodes the object’s shape, so only the readout needs to change. We therefore keep the image-conditioned diffusion transformer (DiT) and ShapeVAE decoder of Hunyuan3D-2 [11] frozen and replace the mesh readout with a lightweight cross-attention head. Its KK learned “convex-slot” tokens query the pretrained shape features, and each emits the HH halfplanes of one convex polytope in the CvxNet formulation [19]; the union of the KK polytopes is the predicted shape. Only this head is trained, with a class-balanced occupancy loss and a convex-pair contrastive loss that suppresses the slab-stacking failure mode of CvxNet-style decoders.

I2CD introduces neither a new convex representation, which we inherit from CvxNet, nor a new 3D generator. Our contribution is to show, and validate for robotics, that a modern image-to-3D foundation model becomes a direct source of simulation geometry with a 38 M-parameter head and under ten GPU-hours of training, inheriting an open-world shape prior that, to our knowledge, no image-to-convex model trained from scratch currently offers. The single-view modes of CvxNet [19] and BSP-Net [20] are category-specific and have no open-world weights (§II). In summary, we:

adapt a frozen Hunyuan3D-2 backbone to emit KK halfplane-parameterized convexes directly, with a training objective that yields compact, spatially coherent parts;

benchmark against eight reconstruct-then-decompose pipelines (Hunyuan3D-2 and SAM3D [13] paired with CoACD, V-HACD, CvxNet, and BSP-Net) on 227227 held-out OmniObject3D [21] and GSO [22] objects, where I2CD achieves the best volumetric IoU at 66–37×37\times lower end-to-end latency;

test simulation-readiness beyond “it loads” with a drop-and-settle study in MuJoCo, PyBullet, Genesis, and Isaac Sim (PhysX): raw generated meshes are silently replaced by a different shape or require costly preprocessing, while I2CD geometry is used as delivered in every engine;

execute 100100 pick-and-place trials on a physical xArm7 in a 2020-object cluttered scene, where I2CD cuts the time to planner-ready scene geometry from 328328 s to 1111 s at comparable execution success (8585 vs. 9090 of 100100).

